    "## **Interpretation of Association Rules**\n",
    "- After running the Apriori algorithm on the 44,000 products, 164 rules were generated. The first self-referencing outputs were identity rules with a lift of 1.0, thereby confirming that these attributes appear frequently and serve as a quick validation check (not meaningful insights).\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "### Sorting by support showed the following:\n",
    "- Men (49.8%), Summer (48.35), and Women (41.9%) are the most common single attributes.\n",
    "- The pair (men, summer) appear in roughly 24% of all products, so we can say that men's summer items are the most prevalent.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Taking into account lift >10:\n",
    "- There is almost a perfect overlap between backpacks and unisex.\n",
    "- There is a clear seasonal marketing trend between Perfume & Body Mist and Spring.\n",
    "- There is a color-season pairing for accessories between Winter & White and Watches.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "### Insights:\n",
    "- We can conclude that the results support the idea that season and gender play a significant role in patterns. The higher the lift, the more meaningful co-occurrences can play when informing recommendation systems. Lower lift identity rules were filtered out in consideration to clarity.\n"
